    title: 'MSc Zoology, Botany, Life Sciences, Environmental Science, or Wildlife Biology',
    body:
      'These stay closest to your BSc. Entrance routes include IIT JAM (Biotechnology and select life-science papers), CUET-PG, and university-specific tests such as DUET at Delhi University. Most programmes expect roughly 55% aggregate in a relevant BSc subject, with relaxation for reserved categories — always verify the exact cutoff on the specific university notification.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Chemistry',
    title: 'MSc Chemistry, Analytical Chemistry, or a Forensic Science specialisation',
    body:
      'Chemistry keeps the widest door open — it feeds directly into CSIR-NET Chemical Sciences, pharma quality roles, and forensic lab entrance requirements. IIT JAM Chemistry and CUET-PG are the common entrance routes.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Biotechnology, Microbiology',
    title: 'MSc Biotechnology, Microbiology, Genetics, or Bioinformatics',
    body:
      'These lead most directly into pharma R&D, genomics, and agri-biotech roles. IIT JAM Biotechnology, CUET-PG, and institute-specific tests such as JNU\'s GAT-B are the usual entrance points. Check whether your BSc subject appears on each programme\'s eligible-subject list before assuming automatic entry.',
  },
];

const researchRealityCards = [
  {
    tone: 'gold',
    title: 'What NET and JRF actually let a fresh BSc graduate do',
    items: [
      'CSIR-NET lets a candidate with a BSc (Hons) or equivalent and at least 55% marks (50% for SC/ST/PwD) apply — but only for the Junior Research Fellowship, not for Lectureship or Assistant Professor eligibility.',
      'To actually draw the JRF fellowship, you must register for a PhD or Integrated PhD programme within the validity window after qualifying — clearing the exam alone does not pay you.',
