  <h2 id="why-different">Why the best career after BSc for PCB students is a different question</h2>

  <p>
    Most PCB career advice online stops at 12th grade.
  </p>

  <p>
    It answers "which degree should I start" and quietly assumes the story ends there.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What that advice misses</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>You already finished the degree decision three or four years ago.</li>
      <li>You now hold an actual BSc — in Biology, Botany, Zoology, Chemistry, Biotechnology, or Microbiology — with real subject depth.</li>
      <li>Your options today are shaped by your marks, your subject, your college, and how much runway you have left before you need income.</li>
      <li>The question is no longer "what should I study" — it is "what do I do with what I already studied."</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      This article is built for that exact stage: after the BSc, before the next commitment.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If your BSc subject is nursing-linked rather than a pure science subject, this article is not the right map for you — nursing careers follow a separate licensing and clinical track.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything here assumes a BSc in a core science subject that a PCB stream commonly leads into.
  </p>

